#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# Cross-demo copy check (K-623, K-607, K-624, K-640).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. The eight demos are eight standalone Rails apps, so shared
# application code is not shared at all — it is HAND-COPIED. `app/services/demo_telemetry.rb`
# ships in seven demos, `app/controllers/demo_activity_controller.rb` in seven,
# `app/services/pow_difficulty.rb` in seven, `app/models/uuid_check.rb` in six, and eleven
# db/migrate files in up to seven. Nothing checked that the copies still agree.
#
# They drift, and the drift is invisible. The telemetry module carried a stale
# `schedule_delivery` comment in six of its seven copies for weeks — corrected
# only by accident, when the K-622 fix happened to touch all seven at once. That
# left the seven byte-identical again, which is a STATE, not a GUARD: the next
# edit that lands in one copy and not the others is exactly as undetectable, and
# the next one may be executable rather than a comment. `script/equihash_register.rb`
# is the proof that it need not be a comment — a constant rename reached three of
# its five copies (057196e) and stopped there, unnoticed, until this check was
# written.
#
# So this is a CHECK, not a convention: it derives every relative path that
# exists in two or more demos, and fails when a copy disagrees with its siblings
# in a way nobody declared. "Someone must remember to propagate the edit" becomes
# "CI notices when they did not". Same shape, and the same reason, as
# bin/check-ci-tasks (K-619) — this is a SIBLING rather than a new mode of that
# script because the two answer different questions from DIFFERENT INPUTS: that
# one compares ci.yml against each demo's rake tasks, this one compares demo
# files against each other, and bin/check-demo-derivations (K-1051) compares one
# demo's narrative document against THAT SAME demo's own producers.
#
# THE INPUT IS THE BOUNDARY, AND IT IS THE ONLY ONE (K-1096). That sentence used
# to end «folding them together would put two unrelated invariants behind one
# exit code and one --write contract», and the reason does not survive its own
# file. MEASURED from the `# ── … ──` banners below: this script now enforces
# EIGHTEEN independent invariants behind exactly one exit code and one --write
# contract — seventeen when that sentence was written, plus T-120's fragment
# rule. Read as a rule about COUNT, the sentence argued for splitting this file
# eighteen ways. It was never about count.
#
# The eighteen share the manifest, the discovery pass and the normalisation, so
# eighteen scripts would re-declare the demo corpus eighteen times — which is
# precisely the drift this file exists to stop. What a sibling may not share is
# its INPUT: a script that compares two different pairs of things cannot tell a
# reader which pair disagreed, and no amount of output formatting fixes that,
# whereas a second invariant over the SAME corpus costs nothing but a section.
#
# The cost of holding eighteen behind one exit code is real and is not argued
# away here: CI cannot distinguish a failing prose claim from a failing
# migration pair without reading the output, and `--write` can rewrite copies
# for a reason the operator did not intend. If that becomes the thing that
# hurts, the split is a T- with a PENDING: clause — a behavioural change to a
# CI gate — and not a header edit.
#
# THE RULE IT ENFORCES. Every relative path found in >= 2 demos must appear in
# the COPIES manifest below with a rule and a reason:
#
#   :identical — byte-for-byte in every demo that ships it.
#   :code      — identical once comments and whitespace are normalised away, so
#                each copy's header prose may name its own demo. Ripper does the
#                stripping, so a `#` inside a string or regex is not a comment.
#                MAGIC comments are the exception and are compared (K-628).
#   :per_demo  — a genuine per-demo implementation that shares only a filename.
#                The FILE is not compared; the manifest entry records WHY they
#                differ, so the next reader does not have to diff seven files to
#                find out. A path here may still have individual METHODS and
#                CONSTANTS held in lockstep — see the T-120 fragment rule below,
#                which exists because «not compared» was letting shared units
#                grow inside these files with nothing watching them.
#
# `ships:` is the OTHER declaration a copy set can carry, and it answers a
# question `except:` cannot: WHICH demos are supposed to have the file at all.
# The lockstep rule compares whatever copies it finds, so a demo that stops
# shipping one — or starts — changes the set silently. K-860 and K-883 are why
# it exists: hoteling's `script/devise_session.rb` and skooti's
# `script/bound_assistant.rb` sat in the tree with ZERO consumers in their own
# demo, held byte-identical to six and three copies that were live, and Phil's
# answer was to delete them. Deleting alone would have left nothing to stop the
# next wave copying them back, because a re-added byte-identical copy is exactly
# what the lockstep rule is happy with. So an entry may name its set exactly:
# a demo that ships the file and is not listed FAILS, and a listed demo that
# does not ship it FAILS too. Entries without `ships:` are unconstrained — the
# declaration is for sets somebody has deliberately reasoned about.
#
# `except:` is the escape hatch for one copy that legitimately differs from its
# siblings (getgrocery's uuid_check declares an extra JSON-Schema constant;
# stylish's stub_user_idp is a DB-backed staff IdP). It is a demo => reason map,
# the same shape as `ungated:` in ci.yml's demos matrix, and it is checked too:
# an exception for a demo that no longer ships the file, or whose copy has since
# converged on its siblings, is an error rather than a comfortable lie.
#
# A path that turns up in a second demo with no manifest entry fails the build.
# That is the property that matters: a NEW copy cannot appear silently, which is
# how six of these got here.
#
# ONE AXIS THE LOCKSTEP RULE CANNOT SEE (K-640). The db/migrate entries keep the
# demos' generator-output migrations identical to EACH OTHER — but those files
# are not hand-written: `rails g kiosk:install` emits them from kiosk-server's
# templates, and all seven demos can drift from the ENGINE together and stay
# green, because they only ever agree with each other. So every install-generator
# template is also declared, in GENERATOR_TEMPLATES below, rendered with the
# generator's own default options (the values every demo was generated with),
# and the render must byte-match every demo copy — regenerating the engine's
# schema without regenerating the demos now fails this check.
#
# AND ONE MORE (K-648). Both rules need a shared handle to grab a file by: the
# lockstep rule matches copies by relative path, the template rule by the
# basename a `.tt` emits. A HAND-WRITTEN migration that ships in two demos at
# demo-specific timestamps has neither — every copy is a singleton path and no
# template renders it — so the pair was compared to NOTHING. Those basenames
# are declared in MIGRATION_PAIRS below and compared across all their copies,
# and an undeclared one fails the build, same as an undeclared path.
#
# WHAT THIS IS NOT. It is not an argument that copying is right. Promoting
# UuidCheck or DemoTelemetry into a shipped gem is a public-API decision (K-607,
# K-495/K-502) and this check does not pre-empt it — it only makes the interim
# state honest, and it is what should be deleted the day the extraction lands.
#
# MAGIC COMMENTS ARE THE ONE COMMENT `:code` STILL COMPARES (K-628). Ripper drops
# every comment, magic ones included, so until this was added a copy that lost
# `# frozen_string_literal: true` compared equal to one that had it — and three of
# the four application_controller.rb copies had lost it, unnoticed, because a
# magic comment is the one comment that changes how the file RUNS. So the leading
# magic-comment block is extracted and compared separately, on the recognised
# Ruby set only (see MAGIC_COMMENTS): prose stays ignored, `# frozen_string_literal`
# does not. `:identical` needs nothing here — byte equality already covers it.
#
# Stdlib only (digest + ripper + erb), no bundle, no database. MEASURED at head
# 2026-08-31: ~1.0 s on a warm checkout, of which about two thirds is the T-117
# citation rule building its ~700-file constant index. It was ~0.2 s — the same
# profile as bin/check-ci-tasks — before that rule existed, and the cost is
# recorded here rather than in a commit message because the next rule that wants
# to walk a tree should know what the last one spent.
#
# Usage:
#   bin/check-demo-copies          # check
#   bin/check-demo-copies --list   # print the manifests as tables

require "digest"
require "erb"
require "ripper"

ROOT = File.expand_path("..", __dir__)

# Where hand-written demo code lives. Deliberately NOT the Rails skeleton
# (bin/, config/, public/, Rakefile, config.ru): those are generator output that
# each demo edits for its own port, host and initializer, and comparing them
# would drown the signal this check exists for. `lib/tasks/*.rake` is outside
# these roots too: a demo's rake file is that storefront's own tasks, and what
# CI runs of them is bin/check-ci-tasks's business. That used to be stated as
# "per-demo by construction, and already covered by bin/check-ci-tasks", and
# K-1043 made both halves false at once (K-1046): the flow-runner helper it
# added is byte-identical in six rake files, and check-ci-tasks compares the
# task INVENTORY, never one demo's rake file against another's. So the file
# stays uncompared and the CONSTRUCT is declared — see the flow-runner rule
# below, which is where any future lockstep fragment in lib/tasks/ belongs.
# What that leaves out, and why, is recorded in SKELETON_NOT_COMPARED below.
SCAN_ROOTS = ["lib", "app", "script", "db/migrate"].freeze

# Named one by one because they are the exceptions: paths outside SCAN_ROOTS
# (or outside `*.rb`) that ARE compared. A path earns a place here either when
# its copies have no per-demo dimension at all (`.gitignore`, the T-048
# statics), or when the single copy that legitimately differs is carried by the
# entry's `except:` map — the six skeleton paths where prove, the API-only
# broker generated on its own, is the sole outlier (K-643).
#
# THE THIRD REASON, and it is the one `spec/` arrives under (T-137): a path can
# be here simply because SCAN_ROOTS cannot see the directory it lives in, so the
# manifest is the ONLY thing that knows the copies exist. `spec/` is not a scan
# root and three demos now ship `spec/wire_arguments_spec.rb`; without this line
# the repo's own rule — «a NEW duplicate fails the build until it is declared» —
# would have been silently untrue for it, which is the same shape as a `:per_demo`
# file whose shared methods nothing compares (T-120).
EXTRA_SCANNED = [
  ".gitignore",
  "spec/wire_arguments_spec.rb",
  "public/robots.txt",
  "public/favicon.ico",
  "public/404.html",
  "public/422.html",
  "public/500.html",
  "config/puma.rb",
  "config/environments/test.rb",
  "config/environments/production.rb",
].freeze

# The one reason six `except:` entries below share (K-643): prove is not a
# divergent COPY of the operator skeleton, it is a different, smaller API-only
# Rails app generated on its own — the recurring shape described atop
# SKELETON_NOT_COMPARED. Stated once so six copies of the sentence cannot
# drift apart.
# The two demos that carry a KYC surface, and the one reason all four of their
# KYC files declare `ships:` (K-657). Stated once so four copies of it cannot
# drift apart, the same way PROVE_SKELETON is.
KYC_DEMOS = %w[getgrocery skooti].freeze
KYC_SHIPS =
  "`ships:` pins the set to getgrocery and skooti deliberately (K-657). Phil has DECIDED that " \
  "`request_kyc`/`kyc_status` do not belong among an operator's business-logic verbs — " \
  "`request_kyc` becomes part of the WIRE and the poll query should not exist at all " \
  "(KYC-WIRE-DIRECTION, 2026-08-12) — and the wire that replaces them is T-062, still unbuilt " \
  "because its design depends on operator-sent events (T-046). So the shape is frozen where it " \
  "was reviewed: a THIRD demo growing a copy would spread a surface already decided against, " \
  "and a copy DISAPPEARING is the T-062 migration itself, which must move this declaration in " \
  "the same wave rather than let it rot."

PROVE_SKELETON =
  "prove is a smaller API-only Rails app generated on its own; its whole skeleton differs from " \
  "the operator seven (the recurring shape noted atop SKELETON_NOT_COMPARED)"

